WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.104
Large context windows are powerful because they let models consider more instructions, source material, conversation history, retrieved content, and tool output during a request.

00:00:12.104 --> 00:00:20.945
They enable workflows involving long documents, codebases, research collections, policy sets, case notes, and multi-step projects.

00:00:20.945 --> 00:00:24.617
But large context is not the same as reliable context.

00:00:24.617 --> 00:00:31.485
Strong context management balances detail against noise, performance, cost, privacy, and reliability.

00:00:31.485 --> 00:00:40.665
It uses clear task statements, source labels, delimiters, priority ordering, compression, summaries, retrieval, provenance, and review.

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:46.309
It treats context length as an operating choice, not a guarantee of better answers.

00:00:46.309 --> 00:00:50.933
Sometimes the best prompt is shorter, sharper, and better organized.

00:00:50.933 --> 00:01:04.942
The main risks are stale information, irrelevant material, conflicting instructions, prompt injection in retrieved content, lost details, overconfident synthesis, and unnecessary exposure of sensitive data.

00:01:04.942 --> 00:01:12.014
These risks grow when users assume that more context automatically solves reasoning and review problems.

00:01:12.014 --> 00:01:18.406
Large windows reduce some friction, but they do not remove the need for engineering judgement.

00:01:18.406 --> 00:01:21.262
The goal is not to fill the entire window.

00:01:21.262 --> 00:01:27.042
The goal is to give the model the right information in a form it can use effectively.

00:01:27.042 --> 00:01:41.119
When teams select context deliberately, preserve provenance, document assumptions, review outputs, and minimize sensitive data, large context windows become a practical tool for deeper, more continuous work.
